# PATENT CLAIM

**Claim 1:** A latching device for electrical relays, comprising:

a) a bistable mechanical latch assembly including a resilient spring member fabricated from a low-force elastic material, said spring member configured to transition between a first stable position corresponding to an open contact state and a second stable position corresponding to a closed contact state, wherein said spring member requires an actuation force substantially lower than conventional relay spring mechanisms to initiate transition between said stable positions;

b) an electromagnetic coil assembly disposed in operative proximity to said bistable mechanical latch assembly, said electromagnetic coil assembly generating a primary magnetic field sufficient to displace said resilient spring member from said first stable position to said second stable position upon energization;

c) a high-voltage discharge circuit operatively coupled to said electromagnetic coil assembly, said high-voltage discharge circuit configured to deliver a transient voltage pulse exceeding the nominal operating voltage of said electromagnetic coil assembly, thereby generating an augmented magnetic field of increased magnitude and reduced rise-time relative to said primary magnetic field;

d) a set of relay contacts mechanically coupled to said bistable mechanical latch assembly, said relay contacts configured to open and close a higher-current electrical circuit distinct from a control circuit energizing said electromagnetic coil assembly;

e) a control circuit configured to trigger said high-voltage discharge circuit in a timed sequence relative to energization of said electromagnetic coil assembly, such that said transient voltage pulse is applied during an initial actuation interval to overcome static friction and inertial resistance of said bistable mechanical latch assembly, and is subsequently discontinued once said latch assembly reaches its target stable position, thereby minimizing continuous power consumption of said electromagnetic coil assembly;

wherein said augmented magnetic field generated by said high-voltage discharge circuit enables said latching device to reliably actuate and control said higher-current electrical circuit using an electromagnetic coil assembly of reduced size, reduced continuous power draw, and reduced material composition relative to a coil assembly required to achieve equivalent actuation force absent said high-voltage discharge circuit.

**Claim 2 (dependent):** The latching device of claim 1, wherein said resilient spring member comprises a beryllium-copper alloy leaf spring having a spring constant selected to permit manual or low-force electromagnetic transition between said first and second stable positions.

**Claim 3 (dependent):** The latching device of claim 1, wherein said high-voltage discharge circuit comprises a capacitor charged to a voltage substantially higher than a supply voltage of said control circuit, and a switching element configured to discharge said capacitor through said electromagnetic coil assembly upon receipt of a triggering signal.

**Claim 4 (dependent):** The latching device of claim 1, wherein said transient voltage pulse has a duration in the range of microseconds to a few milliseconds, sufficient to overcome initial mechanical resistance of said bistable mechanical latch assembly without inducing thermal degradation of said electromagnetic coil assembly.

**Claim 5 (dependent):** The latching device of claim 1, further comprising a sensing element configured to detect arrival of said bistable mechanical latch assembly at said second stable position, and to signal said control circuit to terminate said transient voltage pulse.

**Claim 6 (dependent):** The latching device of claim 1, wherein said relay contacts are configured to switch a current substantially greater than a current flowing through said electromagnetic coil assembly during said high-voltage discharge, such that said latching device functions as a current-amplifying switching mechanism.

**Claim 7 (dependent):** The latching device of claim 1, wherein said bistable mechanical latch assembly remains in said second stable position without continuous application of magnetic force from said electromagnetic coil assembly, thereby eliminating holding current requirements.
